window.SN_INDEX=[{"i": "excel-01", "t": "Getting Started with Excel for Data Analysis", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-01-getting-started-with-excel-for-data-analysis.html", "s": " Why Excel still matters in 2026 Even with Python, SQL, Power BI and AI assistants, Excel is still the first tool almost every analyst opens. It is where business teams keep budgets, targets, trackers and exports from other systems. If you can clean, analyse and present data in Excel quickly, you can add value from day one in any data role. Excel is also the easiest place to learn data thinking. Every idea you meet here comes back later: Idea in Excel Same idea later in the course A table with one header row A SQL table, a pandas DataFrame, a Power BI table SUMIFS, COUNTIFS GROUP BY in SQL, groupby() in pandas, DAX measures VLOOKUP / XLOOKUP SQL JOIN , pandas merge() , Power BI relationships Pivot table SQL aggregation, pivot_table() in pandas, Power BI matrix Conditional formatting Highlighting in dashboards Live class What was covered in class Live class 1 was the programme introduction. Live class 2 covered the basics of Excel, terminology, shortcuts, Excel properties, data types, data validation and formatting, and we started working on the first sheet. This lesson covers the first half; data validation and formatting continue in Lessons 2 and 3. The Excel window, part by part The main parts of the Excel window. The active cell is C4, and the formula bar shows the formula inside it. Part What it does Tip Ribbon Commands grouped into tabs: Home, Insert, Formulas, Data, Review, View Press Alt to see a key letter on every command Name Box Shows the address of the active cell"}, {"i": "excel-02", "t": "Data Entry, Data Validation & Data Cleaning", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-02-data-entry-data-validation-and-data-cleaning.html", "s": " Garbage in, garbage out Most time in real analytics is not spent on fancy charts; it is spent fixing data: extra spaces, inconsistent spellings ( Bangalore , Bengaluru , bengaluru ), numbers stored as text, duplicates and blanks. Surveys of data teams regularly report that cleaning and preparing data takes well over half of an analyst's time. There are two ways to win this fight: Prevent problems at entry time, and clean whatever slips through. Both are needed. Fast, consistent data entry AutoFill and the fill handle Select a cell and drag the small square at its bottom-right corner (the fill handle ). Excel continues the pattern it recognises. You type Drag gives you Jan Feb, Mar, Apr … Monday Tuesday, Wednesday … 1 , 2 (select both) 3, 4, 5 … Q1 Q2, Q3, Q4, Q1 … 01-04-2026 02-04-2026, 03-04-2026 … (right-drag for months or years) Item 1 Item 2, Item 3 … =B2*12 =B3*12 , =B4*12 … Double-click the fill handle to fill a formula down as far as the column next to it has data. This is the fastest way to copy a formula to 10,000 rows. Tip Custom lists File → Options → Advanced → Edit Custom Lists lets you add your own sequences, for example North, South, East, West or your company's department order. AutoFill and custom sort then follow that order. Flash Fill (Ctrl + E) Flash Fill learns a pattern from one or two examples you type. It is perfect for splitting names, extracting codes or reformatting text. X Flash Fill: type the first answer in each column, then press Ctrl + E A B C"}, {"i": "excel-03", "t": "Number, Custom & Conditional Formatting", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-03-number-custom-and-conditional-formatting.html", "s": " Formatting changes how a value looks, not what it is This is the most important idea in this lesson. When you format 0.256 as 26% , the cell still contains 0.256 . Every formula that uses the cell sees 0.256 . X Same stored value, different formats A B C 1 Stored value Format applied What you see 2 0.256 Percentage, 0 decimals 26% 3 0.256 Percentage, 1 decimal 25.6% 4 1,234,568 Number with separator 1,234,567.89 5 46139 Date 27-Apr-2026 6 46139 Long date Monday, 27 April 2026 7 0.6875 Time 16:30 C2 = TEXT (A2, &quot;0%&quot; ) C3 = TEXT (A3, &quot;0.0%&quot; ) C4 = TEXT (A4, &quot;#,##0.00&quot; ) C5 = TEXT (A5, &quot;dd-mmm-yyyy&quot; ) C6 = TEXT (A6, &quot;dddd, d mmmm yyyy&quot; ) C7 = TEXT (A7, &quot;hh:mm&quot; ) Column C uses the TEXT function to show exactly what each format produces. ( TEXT returns text, so use it for labels, not for numbers you still need to calculate with.) Watch out Rounding by format vs rounding by formula Formatting 2.456 with 1 decimal shows 2.5 but keeps 2.456 . Adding three such cells can show 7.4 while the visible numbers add up to 7.5 . If the rounded value must be used in calculations (invoices, tax), round it with a formula: =ROUND(A2,1) . Cell formatting the professional way Open Format Cells with Ctrl + 1 . It has six tabs: Number, Alignment, Font, Border, Fill, Protection. Goal How Bold header with fill colour Home → Bold ( Ctrl + B ) and Fill Color, or Cell Styles → Heading Wrap long text Home → Wrap Text ( Alt + H, W ) Centre a title"}, {"i": "excel-04", "t": "Formulas, Cell References & Aggregate Functions", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-04-formulas-cell-references-and-aggregate-functions.html", "s": " Formula vs function Definition Two words people mix up A formula is any calculation that starts with = . It can use cell references, numbers, operators and functions: =B2*C2 , =(B2-C2)/C2 , =SUM(B2:B10)*1.18 . A function is a named, ready-made calculation inside a formula: SUM , AVERAGE , IF . A function has a name, brackets and arguments separated by commas. Anatomy of a function call. Arguments in square brackets are optional. When you type a function name and ( , Excel shows a tooltip with its syntax, for example SUMIFS(sum_range, criteria_range1, criteria1, [criteria_range2, criteria2], …) . Press Ctrl + A after the bracket to open the Function Arguments dialog, which explains every argument and previews the result. Operators and order of calculation Operator Meaning Example Result + - * / Add, subtract, multiply, divide =10+4*2 18 ^ Power =2^10 1024 % Percent =500*18% 90 &amp; Join text =\"Q\"&amp;2 Q2 = &lt;&gt; &gt; &lt; &gt;= &lt;= Comparison, returns TRUE/FALSE =B2&gt;=100 TRUE or FALSE : Range A1:A10 10 cells , Union (inside functions) =SUM(A1:A3,C1:C3) Adds both ranges Excel follows the usual maths order, remembered as BODMAS/PEMDAS : brackets, then ^ , then * and / , then + and - , then &amp; , then comparisons. X Order of operations A B C 1 Formula Result Why 2 =10+4*2 18 Multiply first: 10 + 8 3 =(10+4)*2 28 Brackets first: 14 × 2 4 =2^3^2 64 Excel works left to right: (2^3)^2 = 64 5 =-2^2 4 Negation before power in Excel: (-2)^2 = 4 6 =100*(1+18%) 118 Add 18% GS"}, {"i": "excel-05", "t": "Text Functions, Logical Functions & IF", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-05-text-functions-logical-functions-and-if.html", "s": " Why text functions matter Real data arrives as text: order IDs like IN-2025-00042 , product codes, email addresses, \"Last, First\" names, addresses and free-text comments. Text functions let you pull out the part you need (the year, the region code, the domain) so you can analyse it. Measuring and extracting text Function What it returns Example Result LEN(text) Number of characters =LEN(\"Pune\") 4 LEFT(text, n) First n characters =LEFT(\"IN-2025-00042\",2) IN RIGHT(text, n) Last n characters =RIGHT(\"IN-2025-00042\",5) 00042 MID(text, start, n) n characters from position start =MID(\"IN-2025-00042\",4,4) 2025 FIND(find, text, [start]) Position of text (case-sensitive, no wildcards) =FIND(\"-\",\"IN-2025-00042\") 3 SEARCH(find, text, [start]) Position (not case-sensitive, wildcards allowed) =SEARCH(\"pune\",\"PUNE-411001\") 1 X Extracting parts of an order ID A B C D E F 1 Order ID Country =LEFT(A2,2) Year =MID(A2,4,4) Order no. =RIGHT(A2,5) Length Year as number 2 IN-2025-00042 IN 2025 00042 13 2025 3 IN-2023-01519 IN 2023 01519 13 2023 4 IN-2024-02988 IN 2024 02988 13 2024 B2 = LEFT (A2, 2 ) C2 = MID (A2, 4 , 4 ) D2 = RIGHT (A2, 5 ) E2 = LEN (A2) F2 = VALUE ( MID (A2, 4 , 4 )) Each pattern is copied down/across to the other cells in its column. Text functions always return text . MID(A2,4,4) returns the text \"2025\" , not the number 2025. Wrap with VALUE() (or multiply by 1) to get a number you can compare or add. When the position changes: FIND + MID Codes rarely have fixed lengths. Combi"}, {"i": "excel-06", "t": "Sorting, Filtering & Dynamic Arrays", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-06-sorting-filtering-and-dynamic-arrays.html", "s": " The sample table We will use this small sales table (sheet Reps , range A1:F9 ) so every result is easy to check by hand. Revenue is =D2*E2 . X Reps: sample sales table A B C D E F 1 Rep Region Product Units Price Revenue 2 Asha North Laptop 5 55,000 275,000 3 Ravi West Phone 12 18,000 216,000 4 Meera South Laptop 3 55,000 165,000 5 Kabir North Tablet 8 25,000 200,000 6 Zoya East Phone 15 18,000 270,000 7 Farhan West Laptop 4 55,000 220,000 8 Neha South Tablet 10 25,000 250,000 9 Rohan North Phone 9 18,000 162,000 F2 =D2*E2 Each pattern is copied down/across to the other cells in its column. Sorting Quick sort Click any cell in a column and use Data → Sort A to Z or Sort Z to A (or Home → Sort &amp; Filter). Excel detects the table around the cell and keeps each row together. Watch out Never sort a single selected column If you select only one column and sort, Excel asks whether to \"expand the selection\". Choosing \"Continue with the current selection\" sorts that column alone and scrambles your data: names no longer match their revenue. Always click a single cell inside the table, or select the whole table. Multi-level (level-wise) sort Data → Sort opens the Sort dialog, where you add levels. Excel sorts by the first level, then breaks ties with the next. The Sort dialog with two levels: Region A to Z, then Revenue largest to smallest. X Result: Region A→Z, then Revenue high→low A B C D 1 Rep Region Product Revenue 2 Zoya East Phone 270,000 3 Asha North Laptop 275,000 4 Kabir"}, {"i": "excel-07", "t": "Lookup Functions: VLOOKUP, XLOOKUP and INDEX-MATCH", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-07-lookup-functions-vlookup-xlookup-and-index-match.html", "s": " Why lookups matter Business data is split across tables: orders in one, products in another, customers in a third. A lookup fetches a value from one table using a key that both tables share, such as a product ID or employee ID. A lookup takes a key from one table, finds it in another table, and returns a value from the same row. Tip You are already learning SQL joins A lookup is a left join for one column at a time: keep every row of the main table and bring in matching information from the other table. You will see the same idea as JOIN in SQL (SQL Lesson 8), merge() in pandas, and relationships in Power BI. We will use this lookup table on a sheet called Prod : X Prod: product lookup table (A1:E7) A B C D E 1 Product ID Product Category Price Stock 2 P101 Office Chair Furniture 4500 32 3 P102 Standing Desk Furniture 18500 8 4 P103 Laser Printer Technology 12900 15 5 P104 Notebook Pack Office Supplies 240 540 6 P105 Smartphone Technology 21999 27 7 P106 Bookcase Furniture 7600 0 VLOOKUP =VLOOKUP(lookup_value, table_array, col_index_num, [range_lookup]) lookup_value : what you are looking for (the key). table_array : the table to search; the key must be in its first column . col_index_num : which column of that table to return (1 = first column). range_lookup : FALSE (or 0) for an exact match. Use TRUE only for approximate matching on sorted bands. X Order lines looking up product details with VLOOKUP A B C D E F 1 Order Product ID Qty Product =VLOOKUP(B2,Prod!$A$2:$E$7,2,FA"}, {"i": "excel-08", "t": "Pivot Tables from Basic to Advanced", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-08-pivot-tables-from-basic-to-advanced.html", "s": " What a pivot table does A pivot table groups rows by one or more fields and summarises a number for each group (sum, count, average…). It is the fastest way to answer \"how much, by what?\" questions, with no formulas. A pivot table turns thousands of order lines into a compact summary: rows grouped by Region, columns by Category, values summed. Creating a pivot table Step by step Step by step Make sure the data is structured (Lesson 1): one header row, no blank rows, no merged cells. Ideally convert it to a Table ( Ctrl + T ) so new rows are included automatically. Click any cell in the data → Insert → PivotTable (or Alt, N, V ). Choose New Worksheet → OK. An empty pivot appears with the PivotTable Fields pane on the right. Drag fields into the four areas: drag region to Rows , category to Columns , sales to Values . Right-click any number → Number Format → #,##0 so the format sticks when the pivot refreshes. The PivotTable Fields pane: a list of fields at the top, four areas at the bottom. Area What it does Example Rows One row per unique value Region, Category, Order Date Columns One column per unique value Year, Segment Values The numbers being summarised Sum of Sales, Count of Order ID Filters Filter the whole pivot by a field Segment = Corporate The pivot cache, Refresh and Refresh All A pivot table does not read your sheet directly. When you create it, Excel copies the data into a hidden pivot cache , and the pivot reads from that cache. This is why pivots are fast, and"}, {"i": "excel-09", "t": "Date, Time & Number Functions and Named Ranges", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-09-date-time-and-number-functions-and-named-ranges.html", "s": " Dates and times are numbers Excel stores a date as the number of days since 1 January 1900 (that day is 1). A time is a fraction of a day : 12:00 noon is 0.5 and 06:00 is 0.25. A date with a time is simply a whole number plus a fraction. X What is really stored A B C 1 You see Stored as Explanation 2 27-04-2026 46139 Days since 1-Jan-1900 3 12:00 0.5 Half a day 4 06:30 0.2708 6.5 hours ÷ 24 5 27-04-2026 18:00 46139.75 Date + time B2 =A2 B3 = TIME ( 12 , 0 , 0 ) B4 = TIME ( 6 , 30 , 0 ) B5 = DATE ( 2026 , 4 , 27 )+ TIME ( 18 , 0 , 0 ) This is why date arithmetic works: X Date arithmetic A B C D E 1 Order date Ship date Days to ship =B2-A2 Due date (+30 days) Overdue? (as of 15-Jun-2026) 2 27-04-2026 02-05-2026 5 27-05-2026 Overdue 3 20-05-2026 21-05-2026 1 19-06-2026 OK 4 01-06-2026 08-06-2026 7 01-07-2026 OK C2 =B2-A2 D2 =A2+ 30 E2 = IF ( DATE ( 2026 , 6 , 15 )&gt;D2, &quot;Overdue&quot; , &quot;OK&quot; ) Each pattern is copied down/across to the other cells in its column. Tip TODAY() and NOW() =TODAY() returns today's date and =NOW() the date and time. They are volatile : they recalculate every time the workbook changes, so results move every day. For reports that must not change, type the \"as of\" date into a cell and refer to it, as in the overdue example above. Shortcuts: Ctrl + ; types today's date as a fixed value. Building and taking apart dates Function Returns Example (A2 = 27-04-2026) Result DATE(y, m, d) A date from parts =DATE(2026,4,27) 27-04-2026 YEAR , MONTH ,"}, {"i": "excel-10", "t": "Charts and Advanced Charts", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-10-charts-and-advanced-charts.html", "s": " Start with the question, not the chart Choose a chart by the question you are answering. Creating and editing a chart Step by step The fast way Select the data including headers (or a summary table / pivot table). For a quick chart press Alt + F1 (embedded) or F11 (new chart sheet). Or use Insert → Recommended Charts to preview options. Click the chart: three buttons appear at its top-right: Chart Elements (+), Chart Styles (brush) and Chart Filters (funnel). Chart Design tab: Switch Row/Column, Select Data, Change Chart Type, Move Chart, Quick Layout. Format pane: double-click any element (axis, series, label) or press Ctrl + 1 with it selected. Element Good practice Title Say the insight: \"Technology drives 77% of sales\", not \"Sales by Category\" Axes Start bar/column axes at zero; format numbers (K, L, %); remove unnecessary decimals Gridlines Light grey or none Data labels Use them instead of an axis when there are few bars Legend Only if there is more than one series; better: label lines directly Colour One highlight colour for the point you want to make, grey for the rest Template Right-click → Save as Template to reuse your style The core chart types Here are the core charts built from Superstore data. The Excel steps follow each one. Column (comparison), line (trend), clustered and stacked bar (comparison of parts), and scatter (relationship). Chart Excel path Use when Clustered column / bar Insert → Column or Bar Comparing categories; use bar when labels are long or "}, {"i": "excel-11", "t": "Interactive Dashboard: Superstore Case Study", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-11-interactive-dashboard-superstore-case-study.html", "s": " The brief Case study Superstore sales review The Head of Sales wants a one-page Excel dashboard to review performance for 2023–2025. They want to see, at a glance: total sales, profit, profit margin and orders; how sales are trending; which regions, categories and sub-categories perform best and worst; and the effect of discounts on profit. They want to filter by year, region and segment. The dashboard will be emailed every month, so it must be protected against accidental changes. Step 0: questions and KPIs first KPI / question Calculation Visual Total sales Sum of sales KPI card Total profit and margin Sum of profit; profit ÷ sales KPI cards Number of orders Distinct count of order_id KPI card Sales trend Sales by month Line chart Regional performance Sales and profit by region Bar chart Product mix Sales by category and sub-category Bar chart / treemap Discount impact Profit by discount level Column chart Filters Year, Region, Segment Timeline + slicers Agreeing on this table before building saves hours of rework. It is also your \"requirements document\" in interviews. Here is the finished result we are aiming for (drawn from the real data): The finished Superstore dashboard: KPI cards on top, trend and breakdown charts below, slicers on the left. Step 1: prepare the data Import superstore_sales.csv with Data → From Text/CSV (Power Query, Lesson 12), or open it and convert the range to a Table named tblSales ( Ctrl + T ). Add helper columns that the pivots will need: Colum"}, {"i": "excel-12", "t": "Power Query, Power Pivot, Analysis ToolPak & What-If Analysis", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-12-power-query-power-pivot-analysis-toolpak-and-what-if-analysi.html", "s": " Why \"Power\" tools? Formulas and pivots are great until the same cleaning has to be repeated every month, the data has several related tables, or it grows past a million rows. The Power tools solve exactly these problems, and they are the same engines used in Power BI , so everything you learn here transfers directly. The modern Excel analytics stack. Power Query and the Data Model are shared with Power BI. Power Query: repeatable cleaning Power Query (Data → Get &amp; Transform Data ) imports data and records every cleaning step you click. Next month, put the new file in place and click Refresh : all steps run again automatically. Step by step Your first query Data → Get Data → From File → From Text/CSV → choose superstore_sales.csv → Transform Data . The Power Query Editor opens. Check data types in each column header (ABC = text, 123 = whole number, 1.2 = decimal, calendar = date). Fix any wrong ones (right-click header → Change Type). Remove columns you do not need (select → right-click → Remove, or Choose Columns ). Filter rows with the header drop-downs (for example remove test orders). Add a column: Add Column → Date → Year , or Custom Column with a formula such as [sales] - [profit] . Rename the query (right panel) to Sales → Home → Close &amp; Load To… → Table, PivotTable report, or Only Create Connection + Add to Data Model . The Power Query Editor. Every action you take is recorded in Applied Steps on the right. Transformations you will use most Transformation Wher"}, {"i": "sql-01", "t": "Databases, RDBMS & SQL Basics", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-01-databases-rdbms-and-sql-basics.html", "s": " What is a database? A database is an organised collection of data stored so that it can be found, updated and analysed efficiently and safely by many people at the same time. A DBMS (database management system) is the software that manages it. Your bank balance, every Swiggy order, every Aadhaar-linked record and every hospital appointment lives in a database. Spreadsheets are personal tools; databases are shared systems built for scale, safety and many users. Types of databases Type Organises data as Examples Typical use Relational (RDBMS) Tables with rows and columns, linked by keys SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle, SQLite Orders, payments, HR, finance: most business data Document JSON-like documents MongoDB, Cosmos DB Product catalogues, app content Key-value Key → value pairs Redis, DynamoDB Caching, sessions Column-family / warehouse Columns, built for analytics Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift, Databricks SQL Analytics on huge data Graph Nodes and edges Neo4j Social networks, fraud rings Vector Number vectors (embeddings) pgvector, Pinecone AI search and RAG (GenAI track) This track focuses on relational databases and SQL , the language almost every data role uses daily. You will practise in Microsoft SQL Server with SSMS , as in class. The SQL you learn transfers to PostgreSQL, MySQL, Snowflake and BigQuery with small differences in functions. How a relational database organises data The hierarchy inside SQL Server: a server holds databases, a database holds schemas"}, {"i": "sql-02", "t": "ER Diagrams, Relationships, Keys & Normalisation", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-02-er-diagrams-relationships-keys-and-normalisation.html", "s": " Why design matters A database that is designed badly is slow, wastes space and, worst of all, lets data contradict itself. Imagine the customer's city stored in every order row: if the customer moves, you must update thousands of rows, and missing one creates two different \"truths\". Good design stores each fact once and connects tables with keys . Entity–Relationship (ER) diagrams An ER diagram is a picture of the data model before any table is created. Definition Building blocks Entity : a real-world thing you store data about: Customer, Order, Product, Employee. Becomes a table . Attribute : a property of an entity: name, city, price. Becomes a column . Relationship : how entities are associated: a Customer places Orders. Implemented with foreign keys . Cardinality : how many of one entity relate to another: one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many. Participation (optionality) : whether the relationship is mandatory (every order must have a customer) or optional (a customer may have zero orders). Crow's-foot notation The most common notation in industry draws symbols at the ends of each relationship line: Crow's-foot symbols. Read each end as \"minimum … maximum\". Reading example: Customer ||——o&lt; Order means \"each order belongs to exactly one customer; each customer places zero or many orders\". Types of relationships One-to-one (1:1) Each row in A matches at most one row in B. Example: Employee ↔ Employee_Bank_Details. Often used to split sensitive or rarely used columns in"}, {"i": "sql-03", "t": "Data Types, Operators, NULLs, Variables & Comments", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-03-data-types-operators-nulls-variables-and-comments.html", "s": " Why data types matter Every column has exactly one data type . The type decides what values are allowed, how much storage they use, how they sort and compare, and which functions work on them. A column of dates stored as text sorts \"01-02-2026\" before \"31-01-2025\" and cannot be used for date arithmetic, the same problem you met in Excel. SQL Server data types The main families of SQL Server data types. Numbers Type Range / precision Storage Use for TINYINT 0 to 255 1 byte Ratings, small codes SMALLINT −32,768 to 32,767 2 bytes Small counts INT about ±2.1 billion 4 bytes IDs, quantities (default choice) BIGINT about ±9.2 × 10¹⁸ 8 bytes Very large IDs, event logs DECIMAL(p, s) / NUMERIC exact, p digits with s after the point 5–17 bytes Money , rates: DECIMAL(12,2) holds up to 9,999,999,999.99 MONEY exact, 4 decimals 8 bytes Currency (DECIMAL is more portable) FLOAT , REAL approximate binary floating point 8 / 4 bytes Scientific measurements, ML scores BIT 0, 1 or NULL 1 bit Yes/No flags Watch out Never store money in FLOAT FLOAT cannot represent many decimal fractions exactly (0.1 + 0.2 is not exactly 0.3), so totals drift by tiny amounts. Use DECIMAL(p, s) for currency. Text Type Description Example CHAR(n) Fixed length, padded with spaces CHAR(2) for state codes MH VARCHAR(n) Variable length, up to n bytes (n ≤ 8000) VARCHAR(100) for names in English NVARCHAR(n) Variable length Unicode (Hindi, Tamil, emoji) NVARCHAR(100) for names in any language VARCHAR(MAX) , NVARCHAR(MAX)"}, {"i": "sql-04", "t": "DDL, DML & Constraints: CREATE, ALTER, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-04-ddl-dml-and-constraints-create-alter-insert-update-delete.html", "s": " The life cycle of a table DDL creates and changes structure; DML fills and changes the rows inside it. CREATE TABLE SQL Copy CREATE TABLE departments ( dept_id INT PRIMARY KEY , dept_name VARCHAR ( 50 ) NOT NULL UNIQUE , location VARCHAR ( 50 ) DEFAULT &#x27;Bengaluru&#x27; ); CREATE TABLE staff ( emp_id INT PRIMARY KEY , full_name VARCHAR ( 100 ) NOT NULL , email VARCHAR ( 100 ) NOT NULL UNIQUE , dept_id INT NOT NULL REFERENCES departments (dept_id), salary DECIMAL ( 10 , 2 ) CHECK (salary &gt; 0 ), join_date DATE DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE, is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE ); Result Commands completed successfully. Commands completed successfully. In SQL Server you would usually write IDENTITY(1,1) for an auto-numbered key, BIT for the flag and DEFAULT GETDATE() for today. Here is the same staff table in full T-SQL style, with named constraints (names make error messages and later changes much easier): SQL Copy CREATE TABLE dbo.staff ( emp_id INT IDENTITY ( 1 , 1 ) NOT NULL , full_name NVARCHAR ( 100 ) NOT NULL , email VARCHAR ( 100 ) NOT NULL , dept_id INT NOT NULL , salary DECIMAL ( 10 , 2 ) NULL , join_date DATE NOT NULL CONSTRAINT df_staff_join DEFAULT ( CAST ( GETDATE () AS DATE )), is_active BIT NOT NULL CONSTRAINT df_staff_active DEFAULT ( 1 ), CONSTRAINT pk_staff PRIMARY KEY (emp_id), CONSTRAINT uq_staff_email UNIQUE (email), CONSTRAINT fk_staff_dept FOREIGN KEY (dept_id) REFERENCES dbo.departments (dept_id), CONSTRAINT ck_staff_salary CHECK (salary &gt; 0 ) ); Constraints"}, {"i": "sql-05", "t": "SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY & Filtering Operators", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-05-select-where-order-by-and-filtering-operators.html", "s": " The shape of a SELECT statement SQL Copy SELECT column_list -- what to show FROM table_name -- where it comes from WHERE row_conditions -- which rows to keep GROUP BY grouping_columns -- (Lesson 7) HAVING group_conditions -- (Lesson 7) ORDER BY sort_columns; -- how to sort the result Only SELECT is required ( SELECT 1 + 1; works). Everything else is optional but must appear in this order. SELECT: columns, expressions and aliases SQL Copy SELECT TOP 5 order_id, order_date, customer_name, sales, profit, sales - profit AS cost, ROUND ( 100.0 * profit / sales, 1 ) AS margin_pct, &#x27;INR&#x27; AS currency FROM superstore; Result order_id order_date customer_name sales profit cost margin_pct currency IN-2023-00001 2023-01-01 Ishaan Gupta 36,000 3,472.55 32,527.45 9.6 INR IN-2023-00001 2023-01-01 Ishaan Gupta 3,850 1,169.54 2,680.46 30.4 INR IN-2023-00001 2023-01-01 Ishaan Gupta 62,080 6,044.47 56,035.53 9.7 INR IN-2023-00002 2023-01-03 Kabir Singh 5,216 450.16 4,765.84 8.6 INR IN-2023-00003 2023-01-03 Sneha Khan 24,591 -5,512.33 30,103.33 -22.4 INR 5 rows × 8 columns SELECT * returns every column. Use it to explore, never in production code (it breaks when columns change and reads more data than needed). An alias renames a column or table in the result: AS margin_pct . AS is optional ( sales - profit cost ) but makes code clearer. Aliases with spaces need brackets or quotes: AS [Margin %] . Expressions can use operators, functions and literals. Table aliases SQL Copy SELECT s.or"}, {"i": "sql-06", "t": "SQL Functions: Aggregate, Text, Date & Number", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-06-sql-functions-aggregate-text-date-and-number.html", "s": " Two kinds of functions Scalar functions return one value per row. Aggregate functions collapse many rows into one value (per group). Aggregate functions Function Returns NULL handling COUNT(*) Number of rows Counts every row COUNT(col) Number of non-NULL values Skips NULLs COUNT(DISTINCT col) Number of distinct non-NULL values Skips NULLs SUM(col) Total Skips NULLs AVG(col) Average of non-NULL values Skips NULLs (they are not treated as 0) MIN(col) , MAX(col) Smallest / largest (numbers, text, dates) Skips NULLs STDEV(col) , VAR(col) Sample standard deviation / variance Skips NULLs STRING_AGG(col, sep) Values joined into one string Skips NULLs SQL Copy SELECT COUNT (*) AS order_lines, COUNT ( DISTINCT order_id) AS orders, COUNT ( DISTINCT customer_id) AS customers, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS total_sales, ROUND ( AVG (sales), 0 ) AS avg_line_sales, MIN (order_date) AS first_order, MAX (order_date) AS last_order, ROUND ( STDEV (sales), 0 ) AS sd_sales FROM superstore; Result order_lines orders customers total_sales avg_line_sales first_order last_order sd_sales 6323 3200 785 201,413,569 31,854 2023-01-01 2025-12-31 60,414 1 rows × 8 columns SQL Copy SELECT category, STRING_AGG (sub_category, &#x27;, &#x27; ) AS sub_categories FROM ( SELECT DISTINCT category, sub_category FROM superstore) AS t GROUP BY category ORDER BY category; Result category sub_categories Furniture Bookcases, Chairs, Tables, Furnishings Office Supplies Paper, Storage, Appliances, Art, Binders Technology A"}, {"i": "sql-07", "t": "GROUP BY, HAVING, CASE WHEN, ROLLUP, CUBE & COALESCE", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-07-group-by-having-case-when-rollup-cube-and-coalesce.html", "s": " GROUP BY: one row per group GROUP BY collapses rows that share the same value(s) into one row, and aggregate functions summarise each group. It is the SQL version of a pivot table (Excel Lesson 8) and of groupby() in pandas. GROUP BY region: rows with the same region are collected into one group, then SUM is computed for each group. SQL Copy SELECT region, COUNT ( DISTINCT order_id) AS orders, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS sales, ROUND ( SUM (profit), 0 ) AS profit, ROUND ( 100.0 * SUM (profit) / SUM (sales), 1 ) AS margin_pct FROM superstore GROUP BY region ORDER BY sales DESC ; Result region orders sales profit margin_pct South 831 53,952,769 4,702,012 8.7 West 811 51,958,521 4,719,833 9.1 North 792 49,018,161 4,555,830 9.3 East 766 46,484,119 4,374,490 9.4 4 rows × 5 columns Grouping by several columns SQL Copy SELECT category, sub_category, COUNT (*) AS lines, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS sales, ROUND ( SUM (profit), 0 ) AS profit FROM superstore GROUP BY category, sub_category ORDER BY category, sales DESC ; Result category sub_category lines sales profit Furniture Tables 505 14,046,224 -816,042 Furniture Bookcases 477 11,274,849 -422,786 Furniture Chairs 493 8,091,474 342,043 Furniture Furnishings 442 1,429,307 78,632 Office Supplies Appliances 492 6,333,529 944,927 Office Supplies Storage 507 2,659,340 397,228 Office Supplies Paper 559 493,232 75,040 Office Supplies Binders 494 401,219 61,074 Office Supplies Art 462 245,985 36,526 Technology Copiers 468 78,749,926 8,436,"}, {"i": "sql-08", "t": "Joins and a Retail Case Study", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-08-joins-and-a-retail-case-study.html", "s": " Why joins? In a normalised database (Lesson 2) customer details live in customers , products in products , and orders in orders and order_items . To answer \"sales by customer segment and product category\" you must join them back together on their keys. A join is SQL's version of XLOOKUP (Excel Lesson 7) and pandas merge() . A tiny example first Two small tables make every join type easy to see: SQL Copy CREATE TABLE students (student_id INT PRIMARY KEY , name VARCHAR ( 20 )); INSERT INTO students VALUES ( 1 , &#x27;Asha&#x27; ), ( 2 , &#x27;Ravi&#x27; ), ( 3 , &#x27;Meera&#x27; ), ( 4 , &#x27;Kabir&#x27; ); CREATE TABLE enrolments (student_id INT , course VARCHAR ( 20 )); INSERT INTO enrolments VALUES ( 1 , &#x27;SQL&#x27; ), ( 1 , &#x27;Python&#x27; ), ( 2 , &#x27;Excel&#x27; ), ( 5 , &#x27;Power BI&#x27; ); SELECT &#x27;students&#x27; AS t, COUNT (*) AS n FROM students UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;enrolments&#x27; , COUNT (*) FROM enrolments; Result Commands completed successfully. (4 rows affected) Commands completed successfully. (4 rows affected) t n students 4 enrolments 4 2 rows × 2 columns Asha (1) has two enrolments, Ravi (2) has one, Meera (3) and Kabir (4) have none, and enrolment for student 5 has no matching student (an \"orphan\" row). The join types as Venn diagrams. Shaded areas are the rows returned (A = left table, B = right table). INNER JOIN: only matches SQL Copy SELECT s.student_id, s.name, e.course FROM students AS s INNER JOIN enrolments AS e ON e.student_id "}, {"i": "sql-09", "t": "Set Operators: UNION, UNION ALL, INTERSECT & EXCEPT", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-09-set-operators-union-union-all-intersect-and-except.html", "s": " Joins add columns; set operators add rows A join puts tables side by side (more columns). A set operator stacks or compares the rows of two query results with the same columns. The four set operators. A and B are the result sets of two queries. Rules for set operators Both queries must return the same number of columns . Corresponding columns must have compatible data types (text with text, numbers with numbers). Column names come from the first query . Only one ORDER BY , at the very end, applies to the combined result. UNION , INTERSECT and EXCEPT remove duplicates; UNION ALL does not. UNION ALL: append UNION ALL stacks results exactly as they are, like Power Query's Append or pasting one table under another. It is the fastest set operator because it does not check for duplicates. SQL Copy SELECT &#x27;Furniture&#x27; AS source, COUNT (*) AS lines, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS sales FROM superstore WHERE category = &#x27;Furniture&#x27; UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;Technology&#x27; , COUNT (*), ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) FROM superstore WHERE category = &#x27;Technology&#x27; UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;Office Supplies&#x27; , COUNT (*), ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) FROM superstore WHERE category = &#x27;Office Supplies&#x27; ORDER BY sales DESC ; Result source lines sales Technology 1892 156,438,413 Furniture 1917 34,841,853 Office Supplies 2514 10,133,304 3 rows × 3 columns A very common real use: combining monthly or regional tables with the same structure. SQL Copy SELECT * FROM sales_j"}, {"i": "sql-10", "t": "Subqueries, Derived Tables & EXISTS", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-10-subqueries-derived-tables-and-exists.html", "s": " What is a subquery? A subquery (inner query, nested query) is a SELECT written inside another statement, in brackets. The outer query uses its result. Where subqueries can appear and what they return. By what it returns Example Used with Scalar (one value) (SELECT AVG(sales) FROM superstore) =, &gt;, &lt; … or as a column Multi-row (one column, many rows) (SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE …) IN , NOT IN , ANY , ALL , EXISTS Table (many columns and rows) (SELECT region, SUM(sales) … GROUP BY region) FROM as a derived table Scalar subqueries \"Which order lines are above the average line value?\" SQL Copy SELECT COUNT (*) AS lines_above_average, ( SELECT ROUND ( AVG (sales), 0 ) FROM superstore) AS average_line_sales FROM superstore WHERE sales &gt; ( SELECT AVG (sales) FROM superstore); Result lines_above_average average_line_sales 1603 31,854 1 rows × 2 columns The inner query runs first and returns one number; the outer query compares each row with it. You cannot write WHERE sales &gt; AVG(sales) directly, because aggregates are not allowed in WHERE (Lesson 5's processing order). \"Show each region's sales and its share of the grand total\": SQL Copy SELECT region, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS sales, ROUND ( 100.0 * SUM (sales) / ( SELECT SUM (sales) FROM superstore), 1 ) AS pct_of_total FROM superstore GROUP BY region ORDER BY sales DESC ; Result region sales pct_of_total South 53,952,769 26.8 West 51,958,521 25.8 North 49,018,161 24.3 East 46,484,119 23.1 4 rows × 3 column"}];